Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns for solving the task (all come from   `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`)  
• `id`: unique station id – return in final list and to join to other tables if needed.  
• `name`: human‑readable station name – return in final list.  
• `latitude` / `longitude`: numerical position of every station – build a GEOGRAPHY point and measure its distance to Chicago’s centre.  
• `state`: two‑letter U.S. state code – optional early filter (`IL`, `IN`, `WI`) to shrink the search space (not strictly required).  
Conditions we will use  
• `latitude IS NOT NULL AND longitude IS NOT NULL` – guarantees we can build points.  
• `ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude), ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) <= 50000` – keeps only stations within 50 km (50 000 m) of the chosen co‑ordinate.  

Below are ten exploratory SQL snippets (each limited to 20 rows) that move from very simple inspection to the final answer.

```sql
-- 1. Description: peek at the first 20 rows of the stations master file.
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: see 20 Illinois stations only (uses the "state" column).
SELECT id, name, latitude, longitude, state
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE state = 'IL'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: rough bounding‑box cut near Chicago (41–43°N,  -88 to -87°W).
SELECT id, name, latitude, longitude
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE latitude BETWEEN 41 AND 43
  AND longitude BETWEEN -88 AND -87
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: compute distance (metres) from Chicago for the same bounding box.
SELECT id,
       name,
       ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude),
                   ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) AS dist_m
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE latitude BETWEEN 41 AND 43
  AND longitude BETWEEN -88 AND -87
ORDER BY dist_m
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: bring in all stations within 100 km, then list the nearest 20.
SELECT id,
       name,
       state,
       ROUND(ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude),
                         ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319))/1000, 2) AS dist_km
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE latitude IS NOT NULL AND longitude IS NOT NULL
  AND ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude),
                  ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) <= 100000
ORDER BY dist_km
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: count how many stations fall inside a 50 km radius.
SELECT COUNT(*) AS stations_within_50km
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE latitude IS NOT NULL AND longitude IS NOT NULL
  AND ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude),
                  ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) <= 50000;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: list distinct state codes represented inside that 50 km circle.
SELECT DISTINCT state
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE latitude IS NOT NULL AND longitude IS NOT NULL
  AND ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude),
                  ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) <= 50000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: same 50 km set but ordered from farthest to nearest (descending).
SELECT id,
       name,
       ROUND(ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude),
                         ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319))/1000, 2) AS dist_km
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE latitude IS NOT NULL AND longitude IS NOT NULL
  AND ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude),
                  ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) <= 50000
ORDER BY dist_km DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: join the 50 km stations to the inventory to check data coverage.
SELECT s.id,
       s.name,
       i.element,
       i.firstyear,
       i.lastyear
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations` AS s
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_inventory` AS i
  ON s.id = i.id
WHERE s.latitude IS NOT NULL AND s.longitude IS NOT NULL
  AND ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(s.longitude, s.latitude),
                  ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319)) <= 50000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: FINAL ANSWER – ids & names of every station ≤ 50 km from Chicago.
SELECT
  id,
  name,
  ROUND(ST_DISTANCE(ST_GEOGPOINT(longitude, latitude),
                    ST_GEOGPOINT(-87.6847, 41.8319))/1000, 2) AS distance_km
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE latitude IS NOT NULL AND longitude IS NOT NULL
